# Project decisions — the *how & why*.
#
# A decision pins an implementation choice over the alternatives that
# were considered. Each decision has:
#   - a stable id (e.g. DEC-001), shared id grammar with requirements
#     and open questions
#   - a status: `accepted` (drives current behavior) or `superseded`
#     (kept as reasoning trail; codegen prompt walks skip these)
#   - a one-line `title` and full `text` body
#   - optional prose: `context` (why we needed to decide),
#     `consequences` (what follows), `alternatives` (what we rejected),
#     `verification` (how we'll know we got it right)
#
# Decisions cross-reference the rest of the spec via:
#   - requirement.decided_by[]   — pin the *how* of a requirement
#   - module.guided_by[]         — inform a module without pinning a req
#   - architecture.project_decisions[] — shape the project as a whole
#   - decisions.toml's own closes_questions[] / defaults_for[] —
#     resolve open questions or commit working defaults
#
# Authoring convention — pick ONE direction per cross-reference:
#   closes_questions[] / closed_by — author one side, not both.
#   defaults_for[]     / default_via — author one side, not both.
# The proposal allows both directions for audit-trail symmetry, but
# graph checks only verify the single direction you author. Authoring
# both forces you to keep them in sync by hand for no extra
# verification benefit. Convention: prefer the OQ-side pointer
# (closed_by / default_via) — it travels with the OQ entry that the
# codegen-gate walk reads, and the decision body stays focused on the
# decision content rather than its bookkeeping.
#
# Supersession keeps both bodies. When DEC-N supersedes DEC-M:
#   - DEC-M.status flips to `superseded`. No forward pointer.
#   - DEC-N (status = accepted) lists `supersedes = ["DEC-M"]`.
# The chain is one-way; the `decision_supersedes_chain_consistency`
# graph check enforces orphan-freedom (every superseded entry is
# pointed at by exactly one accepted entry's `supersedes[]`) and
# acyclicity. The codegen prompt walk follows only `accepted` decisions.
#
# Delete the EXAMPLE entries below once the project has real decisions.

